#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180667 :tutorial/content "How can we subtract fractions, like $\\dfrac{2}{4} - \\dfrac{5}{4}?$\n\nSince both fractions have the same denominator ($4$), we can combine them into one fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{4}-\\frac{5}{4} & =\\frac{2-5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we work out $2-5$ using our number line:\n\n![](images/c6/c63a3db44b79e751c7cf1a49dcebcbb1d00cea0427da4ef271d07646e4be2748.png)\n\nWe get $2-5=-3,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-5}{4}=\\frac{-3}{4}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** If two fractions have different denominators, we need to put them over a common denominator *first* and *then* subtract!" :tutorial/id #uuid "a0129df2-078e-5236-8584-b81858a690fe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3035 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
